## Phase 2: OSS readiness (presentation, not really UX)

- A 30-second demo GIF at the top of the README (create note → AI formatting → bulk TODO import, one flow).
- Screenshots can stay in Japanese UI, but README copy and captions lead in English.

---

## Phase 3: i18n

- Language switch (Japanese/English) lives in the "Account" settings tab. Auto-detected from `Accept-Language` on first visit, changeable afterward.
- Switching updates UI labels immediately (a full page reload is fine; note *content* is never translated — only UI copy).

---

## Phase 4: Organize view

- Clicking the logo at the top of the sidebar opens "Organize" — **not a modal**. The entire main content pane (where the note editor / empty state normally renders) becomes the Organize tree instead, exactly like the editor and the empty state are already two mutually-exclusive states of that same pane. Leaving Organize (a "← Back" link, or clicking any normal sidebar link) is an ordinary navigation back to wherever you were, not a "close" with state to preserve.
- Scope, corrected from the original draft of this section: **notebooks, folders, and notes are all tree nodes**, each renamable/deletable/movable — a version scoped to "notebooks and folders only" shipped first but turned out to make the app impossible to fully organize (there was nowhere to move a note into a different folder at all). Note *display order* stays out of scope regardless — the existing pin + 3-button sort (above) remains authoritative; dragging a note in the tree only ever changes its folder, never a position.
- Notebook/folder creation is available from inside Organize; note/file creation stays sidebar-only.
- The regular editor never pays the drag-and-drop cost — heavy code only loads when the Organize view actually renders (a real conditional server-render, not a hidden-but-present modal, so this falls out naturally rather than needing an explicit lazy-load trigger).

---

## Phase 5: Researcher-only options (off by default)

### DOI / citation metadata

- Pasting a long DOI (e.g. `https://doi.org/10.3390/pr14020243`) into a note body is detected; a small chain-link icon floats at line end. Clicking it offers:
  - Insert a formatted citation pulled from CrossRef/OpenAlex (title, authors, journal)
  - **Convert to the short form via the Short DOI Service (shortdoi.org)** for slides (e.g. `https://doi.org/rjjq`) and copy it to the clipboard
  - Both options live in the same dropdown off the same icon — don't add a new button per feature.
- No Web of Science integration — no free API, and most users couldn't reach it anyway.

### Zotero integration

- Lives inside the "Integrations (researcher)" settings tab, off by default. Until enabled, nothing Zotero-related appears anywhere in the editor.

---

## Additional candidates for the top two personas

### Researchers / grad students / engineers

- **Character/word counter with selection support**: small, always-visible counter under the editor. **With nothing selected it counts the whole document; select text and it switches to counting just the selection** — built for pulling one relevant chunk out of a pasted AI conversation without the surrounding noise skewing the count. Shows Japanese character count and English word count side by side (`1,234 chars / 210 words`) — fixes the gap where existing browser extensions don't handle English word counts correctly.
- **BibTeX export**: pairs with DOI/citation metadata (Phase 5) — one button to also copy the fetched citation as `.bib`. A natural extension for LaTeX users.

### AI-meeting-notes users (consulting/PM/sales)

- **Action-item extraction from free text**: detect lines that read like "...will do X" or "TODO:" and surface a low-key inline suggestion, "Convert to TODOs?" A natural extension of the bulk-import modal from Phase 1 — reuse the same UI rather than building a new one.
- No fixed meeting-note template. Templates constrain more than they help over time (learned the hard way keeping a research notebook to a template — it stops you from writing what actually matters). Free-form text plus the action-item suggestion above is the whole feature.

---

## PWA

- "Add to Home Screen" gets you an app icon and full-screen launch — installability only, no offline sync.
- **Offline switches to read-only mode.** On detecting a dropped connection, a persistent banner appears: "Offline (read-only)." Recently visited notes stay readable from the Service Worker cache; every editing control (save button, TODO checkboxes, input fields) gets disabled.
- Addresses the real case of an unreliable university conference-room Wi-Fi: being unable to even *read* your notes is the failure mode to avoid. Writes are refused until the connection returns — no write-queue machinery.
